cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Domain-Specialized Byte-Level BPE for Technical Documentation: Compression Gains and General-English Tradeoffs"
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it using these metadata."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: "Johnson"
    family-names: "Shi"
    email: "johnsonshi86@gmail.com"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0001-2638-6007"
    affiliation: "Microsoft"
repository-code: "https://github.com/johnsonshi/azure-domain-tokenization"
url: "https://github.com/johnsonshi/azure-domain-tokenization"
abstract: >-
  A controlled, CPU-only case study measuring how training-corpus composition
  changes byte-level BPE tokenizer compression on unseen Azure, other Microsoft,
  external technical (Kubernetes), and general-English documentation. Under a
  fixed 32 MiB byte-matched training budget and matched 16K vocabulary, an
  Azure-trained tokenizer used 36.68% fewer tokens per 1,000 UTF-8 bytes than a
  matched General tokenizer on a cross-service Azure holdout, while using 34.75%
  more tokens on general English; a 50/50 Mixed tokenizer retained 93.3% of the
  in-domain gain while using only 4.39% more tokens on general English. Measures
  tokenizer compression and CPU tokenization throughput only; no model-quality,
  latency, GPU-throughput, or cost claims.
keywords:
  - tokenization
  - byte-level BPE
  - domain adaptation
  - text compression
